<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<refentry xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.2"
          xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"
          xml:id="ndm_driftcorrect">

  <info>
    <productname>neurosuite-3</productname>
    <authorgroup>
      <author><personname><firstname>neurosuite-3</firstname>
        <surname>contributors</surname></personname></author>
    </authorgroup>
    <copyright><year>2025</year><holder>neurosuite-3 contributors</holder></copyright>
    <legalnotice><para>GPL-3.0-or-later</para></legalnotice>
  </info>

  <refmeta>
    <refentrytitle>ndm_driftcorrect</refentrytitle>
    <manvolnum>1</manvolnum>
  </refmeta>

  <refnamediv>
    <refname>ndm_driftcorrect</refname>
    <refpurpose>
      Geometrically refeaturize spike waveforms to a reference probe position
    </refpurpose>
  </refnamediv>

  <refsynopsisdiv>
    <cmdsynopsis>
      <command>ndm_driftcorrect</command>
      <arg choice="opt"><replaceable>session</replaceable></arg>
    </cmdsynopsis>
  </refsynopsisdiv>

  <refsect1>
    <title>Description</title>
    <para>
      <command>ndm_driftcorrect</command> applies a per-probe drift signal
      (<filename>SESSION.dat.drift.P</filename>, produced by
      <command>ndm_estimatedrift</command>) to the per-spike waveform
      files of every spike-detection group, producing drift-corrected
      waveform files <filename>SESSION.spkC.N</filename> (or
      <filename>SESSION.spkCD.N</filename> for the stderiv pipeline)
      in which every spike has been spatially reinterpolated as if the
      probe were sitting at the reference position
      <emphasis>d(t)=0</emphasis>.
    </para>
    <para>
      For spike <emphasis>i</emphasis> at timestamp
      <emphasis>t<subscript>i</subscript></emphasis> with probe drift
      <emphasis>d<subscript>i</subscript></emphasis> µm, site k at
      reference depth z<subscript>k</subscript> physically sat at depth
      z<subscript>k</subscript>+d<subscript>i</subscript> when the spike
      was recorded.  The correction interpolates the recorded values
      back to the reference grid:
      <programlisting>
s_k_corrected(t) = interp(z_k − d_i, {z_j}_j, {s_j(t)}_j)
      </programlisting>
      For drifts smaller than one site spacing this is well-conditioned
      and sub-µm accurate.  For larger drifts the interpolation clamps
      to boundary site values (<function>numpy.interp</function>
      semantics) and emits a warning.
    </para>
    <para>
      <emphasis role="strong">This plugin reads no
      <filename>.clu</filename> file.</emphasis>  The correction is
      purely geometric.  Whether a spike was labelled artifact (cluster
      0), MUA (cluster 1), or assigned to a real unit makes no
      difference: the recorded waveform is the same physical signal,
      and so is the corrected waveform.
    </para>
    <para>
      This decoupling enables iterative refinement.  A group whose
      spikes all landed in clusters 0/1 in iteration N can recover real
      units in iteration N+1 after refeaturization, because
      refeaturization did not gate on the (provisional) cluster
      labels.  As long as <emphasis>some</emphasis> shank on the same
      probe was curated well enough to drive
      <command>ndm_estimatedrift</command>, every sibling shank on
      that probe gets refeaturized using the propagated probe-level
      drift signal — including shanks whose own
      <filename>.clu</filename> contains nothing but noise and MUA.
    </para>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1>
    <title>Workflow position</title>
    <programlisting>
ndm_extractspikes(_stderiv)  →  .spk(D).N
ndm_pca(_stderiv)            →  .fet(D).N
ndm_klustakwik               →  initial .clu.N (rough; drift not yet corrected)
ndm_estimatedrift            →  .drift  +  .dat.drift.P  (per probe)
   ↓
<emphasis role="strong">ndm_driftcorrect</emphasis>             →  .spkC(D).N
   ↓
ndm_pca(_stderiv) [re-run]   →  .fetC(D).N
ndm_klustakwik [re-run]      →  refined .clu.N
ndm_estimatedrift [re-run]   →  sharper drift estimate
   ↓
… iterate until max |Δd(t)| converges below threshold …
    </programlisting>
    <para>
      The drift signal is the <emphasis>absolute</emphasis> offset
      between the probe and the reference position, not the increment
      between successive iterations.
      <command>ndm_driftcorrect</command> always reads the original
      <filename>.spk(D)</filename> (never a previous
      <filename>.spkC(D)</filename>) so successive iterations do not
      compound interpolation error.  Setting
      <parameter>overwrite</parameter> to <literal>true</literal>
      forces the output to be regenerated when the drift signal has
      been refreshed.
    </para>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1>
    <title>Parameters</title>
    <para>
      All parameters are optional.  Defaults shown in parentheses.
      Read from the <literal>ndm_driftcorrect</literal> programs block
      in the YAML parameter file.
    </para>
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>overwrite</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          When <literal>true</literal>, regenerate
          <filename>.spkC(D)</filename> outputs that already exist.
          Default: <literal>false</literal>.  Set this when re-running
          after a refreshed <filename>.dat.drift.P</filename>.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>passthroughThreshUm</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          When the maximum absolute drift across the recording is
          below this threshold (µm), the input
          <filename>.spk(D)</filename> is byte-copied to the
          <filename>.spkC(D)</filename> output instead of being
          re-interpolated.  Default: <literal>0.25</literal>.  Avoids
          unnecessary computation on probes that did not drift.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>batchSize</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Number of spikes processed per memory-resident batch.
          Default: <literal>100000</literal>.  The
          <filename>.spk(D)</filename> file is memory-mapped, so the
          batch size only bounds the working-set RAM of the
          interpolation kernel — at <literal>100000</literal> spikes
          × 82 samples × 384 channels × 4 B/float ≈ 12 GB the kernel
          stays comfortably below typical workstation RAM.  Lower this
          on small-RAM systems; raise it for slightly faster
          throughput on large-RAM systems where the kernel can
          process more spikes per launch overhead.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>nWorkers</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Number of worker processes for group-parallel drift
          correction.  Default <literal>1</literal> (sequential).
          Set to <literal>0</literal> to use all available CPUs, or
          to a positive integer to cap the worker count.  Each group
          is processed independently — its own
          <filename>.res</filename>, memory-mapped
          <filename>.spk(D)</filename>, memory-mapped
          <filename>.dat.drift.P</filename>, and own output file —
          so throughput scales nearly linearly with group count up
          to physical core count.  For NPX-class data where each
          group's interpolation kernel does seconds of work, this is
          a near-free speedup; for octrode-class data with milliseconds
          of work per group, the spawn overhead can dominate and
          sequential mode is faster.
        </para>
        <para>
          Output files are deterministic in content: each group writes
          to its own <filename>.spkC(D).N</filename> path, and the
          interpolation is purely a function of the input
          waveforms and drift signal.  Two runs at different
          <parameter>nWorkers</parameter> values produce byte-identical
          output files for every group.  Only the order of stderr
          progress messages differs.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>probeLibrary</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Override probe-library search path.  Same semantics as
          <command>ndm_estimatedrift</command>.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1>
    <title>Edge cases &amp; how they are handled</title>
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Source group's <filename>.clu</filename> contains only
              clusters 0 and/or 1</term>
        <listitem><para>
          <command>ndm_estimatedrift</command> returned no estimate
          for that group — and if no sibling on the same probe was
          curated either, no <filename>.dat.drift.P</filename> was
          written.  <command>ndm_driftcorrect</command> skips the
          probe with an info message; downstream tooling falls back
          to the uncorrected <filename>.spk(D)</filename>.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Target group's <filename>.clu</filename> contains only
              clusters 0 and/or 1</term>
        <listitem><para>
          The group is refeaturized normally using the propagated
          probe-level drift signal.  Re-clustering the corrected
          waveforms may surface units that were buried in MUA before.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Drift signal is essentially zero
              (max(|d|) &lt; <parameter>passthroughThreshUm</parameter>)</term>
        <listitem><para>
          Fast-path: <filename>.spk(D)</filename> is byte-copied to
          <filename>.spkC(D)</filename>.  No interpolation runs.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>NaN-depth sites in inline
              <literal>sitePositions_um</literal></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Those sites pass through unchanged on the output, AND are
          excluded from the interpolant.  Valid sites are
          interpolated normally.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Drift exceeds half the array span</term>
        <listitem><para>
          Boundary clamp.  A warning is printed listing the spike
          count for which this happened — typically indicates an
          outlier window in the drift estimate that should be reviewed.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Spike timestamp out of
              <filename>.dat.drift.P</filename> bounds</term>
        <listitem><para>
          Clamped to the first/last sample of the drift signal — same
          semantics as <function>numpy.interp</function> and consistent
          with how <command>ndm_estimatedrift</command> writes the
          binary signal.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Group has no probe geometry
              (no <literal>sitePositions_um</literal>, no probe file)</term>
        <listitem><para>
          Skipped with an info message.  Without depth coordinates the
          interpolation cannot run.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Output <filename>.spkC(D)</filename> already exists</term>
        <listitem><para>
          Skipped unless <parameter>overwrite</parameter> is
          <literal>true</literal>.  Set
          <parameter>overwrite</parameter> when iterating with a
          refreshed drift signal.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1>
    <title>Files</title>
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.yaml</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>Session parameter file (mandatory input).</para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.res.N</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Spike timestamps (mandatory input — used to look up per-spike
          drift values).
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.spk.N</filename> /
              <filename>SESSION.spkD.N</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Recorded waveforms (mandatory input).  When both are present
          <filename>.spkD.N</filename> is preferred (matches the
          stderiv pipeline used by <command>ndm_pca_stderiv</command>).
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.dat.drift.P</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Per-probe drift signal (int16 µm, one sample per recording
          sample), produced by <command>ndm_estimatedrift</command>.
          P is the 1-based probe index in the YAML
          <literal>probes</literal> list.  At least one such file must
          exist; groups whose probe lacks a drift signal are skipped.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.spkC.N</filename> /
              <filename>SESSION.spkCD.N</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Drift-corrected waveform output.  Same on-disk layout as the
          input (int16 sample-major).
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1>
    <title>Wiring into the pipeline</title>
    <para>
      Until <command>ndm_pca</command> /
      <command>ndm_pca_stderiv</command> /
      <command>ndm_klustakwik</command> grow native
      <filename>.spkC(D)</filename> awareness (see the
      <function>pickInputPath</function> helper in
      <command>KlustaKwik</command>), the corrected files can be
      consumed by symlink or by passing explicit paths to the
      individual tools.  The recommended pattern for an iterative
      refinement loop is:
    </para>
    <programlisting>
# 1) Initial pass — no drift correction yet.
ndm_extractspikes_stderiv  $session
ndm_pca_stderiv            $session
ndm_klustakwik             $session

# 2) First drift estimate.
ndm_estimatedrift          $session
ndm_driftcorrect           $session

# 3) Refeaturize and recluster on .spkCD.
for g in $groups; do
    mv $session.spkD.$g  $session.spkD.$g.iter0
    cp $session.spkCD.$g $session.spkD.$g    # let ndm_pca_stderiv see it
done
ndm_pca_stderiv            $session
ndm_klustakwik             $session

# 4) Sharpen drift estimate.
ndm_estimatedrift          $session
# Compare drift signals; iterate if max|Δd(t)| above threshold.
    </programlisting>
  </refsect1>

